* ===========================================================================
*  CP/M-over-serial, increment 3: BOOT ROM (the "hardware boot").
*
*  Models what the drive/controller did when a disk was present and no operating
*  system was running (see .kiro/specs/cpt8000-emulator/boot-sequence.md): read the
*  boot sector, and if the disk is usable, load it and run it.  If there is no disk
*  (or the read fails) keep retrying, as the hardware did, so a disk inserted later
*  gets booted.
*
*  Runs from reset at 0000H.  Reads block 0 of drive 1 into memory at 0100H and
*  jumps to it.  Must stay below 0100H in size.
*
*  Disk controller ports (placeholder protocol; [VERIFY] vs. real hardware):
*     80  STATION   out: 0 = drive 1, 1 = drive 2
*     81  BLOCK_LO  out: low byte of the block number
*     82  BLOCK_HI  out: high byte of the block number
*     83  COMMAND   out: 1 = read      /  STATUS in: bit 0 = OK, bit 7 = no media
*     84  DATA      in : next byte of the block just read
*
*  Block number = track*26 + (sector-1) on the 77x26x128 geometry; the boot
*  sector is block 0.  [VERIFY] which track/sector the real hardware read.
* ===========================================================================
DSKSTN    EQU 80
DSKLO     EQU DSKSTN+1
DSKHI     EQU DSKSTN+2
DSKCMD    EQU DSKSTN+3
DSKDATA   EQU DSKSTN+4
CMDREAD   EQU 1
DSKOK     EQU 1
*
          ORG 0
*
*  A stack, high in bank 0, in case the boot sector wants to CALL.
BOOT      32512:HL
          HL:SP
*
*  Ask drive 1 for block 0.
RETRY     0:A
          A:PORT(DSKSTN)
          A:PORT(DSKLO)
          A:PORT(DSKHI)
          CMDREAD:A
          A:PORT(DSKCMD)
*
*  Status bit 0 clear means no media / failed read: try again.
          PORT(DSKCMD):A
          A+*DSKOK:A
          FZ=1? /RETRY
*
*  Stream the 128 bytes of the block to 0100H.
          256:HL
          128:B
RDLOOP    PORT(DSKDATA):A
          A:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          B-1:B
          FZ=0? /RDLOOP
*
*  Hand over to the boot sector.
          /256
